<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Lausanne</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1923">1923</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkish-war-of-independence">Turkish War of Independence</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a>, signed on 24 July 1923, settled the conflict between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> and the Turkish nationalist government, replacing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sevres">Treaty of Sèvres</a>. It recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>'s borders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, including Smyrna, confirmed Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> islands and Italian possession of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodecanese">Dodecanese</a>, and accompanied the Greek–Turkish population exchange.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lausanne.jpg" alt="Treaty of Lausanne" width="320" height="164" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkey-Greece-Bulgaria_on_Treaty_of_Lausanne.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The treaty was negotiated at the Lausanne Conference of 1922 to 1923 and signed in the Palais de Rumine in Lausanne on 24 July 1923. It was a second attempt at peace after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sevres">Treaty of Sèvres</a> of 1920, which had sought to partition Ottoman territories but was never ratified. The Turkish National Movement rejected its terms.</p><p>After the Greek defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-turkish-war-1919-1922">Greco-Turkish War</a>, Turkish forces recaptured İzmir, and the Armistice of Mudanya of October 1922 provided for an exchange of Greek and Turkish populations and for civilian passage through the Turkish Straits. The conference opened on 20 November 1922 and took eight months of negotiation, with several Turkish withdrawals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismet-inonu">İsmet İnönü</a> was chief negotiator for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">Lord Curzon</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleftherios-venizelos">Eleftherios Venizelos</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty had 143 articles and was written in English and French. It provided for the independence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey</a>, and for the protection of the Greek Orthodox minority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and the Muslim minority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Most of both populations had already been moved under the earlier convention on the exchange of populations; only the Greek Orthodox of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imbros">Imbros</a> and Tenedos, about 270,000 people, and the Muslim population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-thrace">Western Thrace</a>, about 129,120 in 1923, were excluded.</p><p>Islands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> beyond three miles from the Turkish shore went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imbros">Imbros</a>, Tenedos and Rabbit Island. Turkey renounced its claims to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodecanese">Dodecanese</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, and left the fate of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>. Greece gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> in exchange for ceding Karaağaç. Annex VIII, the Declaration of Amnesty, granted legal immunity for acts committed between 1914 and 1922 on account of military or political action, which ended efforts to prosecute Ottoman war criminals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> ratified the treaty on 23 August 1923 and all other signatories had done so by 16 July 1924. It took effect on 6 August 1924. It brought international recognition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey</a> as successor to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, and the Ottoman public debt was divided between Turkey and the states that emerged from the empire.</p><p>The convention on the Straits lasted thirteen years until the Montreux Convention of 1936. For <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, the treaty ended the impetus behind the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megali-idea">Megali Idea</a>. David Lloyd George called it an abject, cowardly and infamous surrender. Historians such as Norman Naimark describe it as a pivotal precedent for transferring populations against their will in the 20th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Lausanne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The defeat of 1922 and the exchange of populations agreed with Turkey at <b>Lausanne</b> in 1923, which brought over a million Orthodox Christians to Greece and sent some 400,000 Muslims to Turkey, ended some three thousand years of Greek life in Asia Minor and became a model, much…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Lausanne</b> of 24 July 1923 superseded Sèvres and recognised the Turkish state as the empire's successor; the last Allied troops left Istanbul on 4 October, and on 29 October 1923 the Republic was proclaimed in Ankara, the new capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It is a recognised minority language in Cyprus, Hungary, Iraq, Poland and Romania, and in Turkey it is protected by the 1923 <b>Treaty of Lausanne</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/armenian">Armenian · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The assembly abolished the Ottoman sultanate in November 1922, won recognition in the <b>Treaty of Lausanne</b> of July 1923, and proclaimed the Republic of Turkey on 29 October 1923.</blockquote><a href="/state/government-of-the-grand-national-assembly">Government of the Grand National Assembly</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Ouchy in 1912 made the occupation provisional, but Italy kept the islands through the First World War, and the <b>Treaty of Lausanne</b> in 1923 recognised its sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/state/italian-islands-of-the-aegean">Italian Islands of the Aegean</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek forces took it in 1912, and the <b>Treaty of Lausanne</b> and the Greek constitution of 1927 confirmed its self-government under Greek sovereignty, with a civil governor.</blockquote><a href="/state/monastic-community-of-mount-athos">Monastic Community of Mount Athos</a></li></ul></section>
